VERDICT
Correct: your code correctly filters negative numbers and reverses the integer to check for equality, using long long to avoid integer overflow.

REVIEW MY CODE

What I did well:

You eliminated negative numbers immediately with if(x < 0) return false;, knowing that a negative sign at the front cannot appear at the back.

You used long long for rev_num to safely handle reversed numbers that would otherwise overflow a standard 32-bit signed integer.

You extracted and shifted digits correctly using % 10 and * 10 + digit.

Is it correct?

Yes, it passes all test cases on LeetCode.

Minor code simplification: lines 15 through 19 can be simplified from if(rev_num == x) return true; else return false; directly to return rev_num == x;.

Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses:

There are no logical bugs under LeetCode constraints.

However, relying on a 64-bit integer (long long) is often viewed as bypassing the core challenge in interviews where 64-bit integers are explicitly prohibited.

Edge cases handled properly: negative numbers (returns false), single digits 0 through 9 (returns true), and numbers ending in 0 like 10 (returns false).

Time and space complexity of MY code, each with a one-line reason:

Time complexity: O(log10(x)) because the while loop divides the input by 10 on each iteration, visiting every digit once.

Space complexity: O(1) auxiliary space because only a few fixed-size scalar variables (num, rev_num, digit) are used.

Why my approach works:

Take x = 121. The loop pops digits from the back of 121 and builds rev_num: 1, then 12, then 121. Since 121 == 121, it confirms the number is symmetrical and returns true.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: Convert to String and Two Pointers (Brute Force)

Where it comes from:

Standard beginner baseline found across LeetCode community discussions.

Idea:

Convert the integer to a string using std::to_string. Place a pointer at the beginning and one at the end, moving toward each other and checking if characters match.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <string>

class Solution {
public:
    bool isPalindrome(int x) {
        if (x < 0) {
            return false;
        }
        std::string s = std::to_string(x);
        int left = 0;
        int right = s.length() - 1;
        while (left < right) {
            if (s[left] != s[right]) {
                return false;
            }
            left++;
            right--;
        }
        return true;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(log10(x)) because creating and traversing the string depends on the number of digits.

Space Complexity:

O(log10(x)) auxiliary space to store the string on the heap.

What makes the next approach better:

Converting to a string allocates extra memory. We can extract digits mathematically without string conversion.

Approach 2: Full Integer Reversal Using 64-bit Integer (Your Approach)

Where it comes from:

Classic arithmetic digit extraction found in introductory textbooks and GeeksforGeeks.

Idea:

Peel off digits from right to left using % 10 and / 10, assemble the reversed integer into a 64-bit container (long long) to prevent overflow, and compare it with the original number.

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    bool isPalindrome(int x) {
        if (x < 0) {
            return false;
        }
        int num = x;
        long long rev_num = 0;
        while (num != 0) {
            rev_num = rev_num * 10 + (num % 10);
            num /= 10;
        }
        return rev_num == x;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(log10(x)) because each loop step peels off one decimal digit.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space using only primitive numbers.

What makes the next approach better:

We can stop reversing halfway through the number. Reversing only half prevents any chance of integer overflow, avoiding 64-bit integers entirely.

Approach 3: Reversing Only Half the Number (Most Optimised)

Where it comes from:

Official LeetCode editorial and canonical interview solution.

Idea:

Since a palindrome is mirrored, the second half reversed will equal the first half. We keep peeling digits into rev until x <= rev. For odd-length numbers like 12321, x will be 12 and rev will be 123, so we drop the middle digit with rev / 10. Numbers ending in 0 (except 0 itself) can never be palindromes.

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    bool isPalindrome(int x) {
        if (x < 0 || (x % 10 == 0 && x != 0)) {
            return false;
        }
        int rev = 0;
        while (x > rev) {
            rev = rev * 10 + (x % 10);
            x /= 10;
        }
        return x == rev || x == rev / 10;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(log10(x) / 2) which simplifies to O(log10(x)), running half as many iterations as full reversal.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space strictly using standard 32-bit signed integers.

WHERE I STAND

Which approach did I use?

You used Approach 2 (Full Integer Reversal Using 64-bit Integer).

Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem?

Interviewers expect Approach 3 (Reversing Only Half the Number). They specifically check if you can solve the problem without converting to a string and without using long long to avoid integer overflow.

Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:

Question 1: What if the environment strictly forbids 64-bit integer types like long long?

Answer: Reverse only the second half of the number. The reversed half will only have half the total digits, so it cannot exceed the square root of the maximum integer, making overflow impossible.

Question 2: Why do you need the check (x % 10 == 0 && x != 0)?

Answer: For a number like 10, the loop would stop after one iteration (x = 1, rev = 0), and x == rev / 10 would compare 1 == 0, but without the check, non-palindromic multiples of 10 might produce matching front and back values when zeros are stripped.

Question 3: Can you solve it by comparing the first and last digits directly without modifying x?

Answer: Yes, by finding the largest power of 10 that divides x (e.g. 100 for 121), comparing x / divisor with x % 10, and then trimming both ends. However, half-reversal is simpler and less prone to off-by-one errors.

WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER (for my revision notes)

The key insight, in one sentence:

A palindrome is symmetric, so reversing just the second half of the number is enough to verify equality without risking integer overflow.

My mistakes, as a short checklist for next time:

Avoid return true/false inside if-else blocks; simplify directly to return condition;.

Before reaching for long long to avoid overflow, check if you can terminate the algorithm earlier.

Always verify numbers ending in 0 when doing half-number digit processing.

The pattern or idea this question is really about:

Digit extraction using modulo arithmetic and two-way convergence without extra space.

